Title: Numerical study of the drag force, interfacial area and mass transfer in bubbles in a vertical pipe
Fetched: 2026-09-05T14:40:14.388Z

# Numerical study of the drag force, interfacial area and mass transfer in ✩ bubbles in a vertical pipe

Néstor Balcázar-Arciniega<sup>∗</sup>, Joaquim Rigola, Carlos D. Pérez-Segarra, Assensi Oliva

*Heat and Mass Transfer Technological Center, Heat Engines Department, Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya-BarcelonaTech, Colom 11, Terrassa*
*(Barcelona), 08222, Spain*

## A R T I C L E I N F O

*Keywords:*
Bubbles
Drag force
Mass transfer
Unstructured conservative level-set method
(UCLS)
Unstructured flux-limiters
Unstructured meshes
Finite-volume method

## A B S T R A C T

A systematic numerical study of drag force and mass transfer in gravity-driven bubbles in a vertical pipe is
performed at Re ∼ *𝑂*(100 − 1000). This research employs a parallel multi-marker unstructured conservative
level set method for interface capturing, avoiding the numerical coalescence in bubble swarms. The finite
volume method discretizes transport equations on 3D collocated unstructured meshes. Unstructured flux limiter
schemes solve the convective term of transport equations, preserving the numerical stability at high Reynolds
numbers and high-density ratios. Thermodynamic equilibrium is assumed at the interface for the concentration
of chemical species. The hydrodynamics and mass transfer of bubbles are validated against classical correlations
from the literature. Finally, direct simulations are executed to develop new correlations for the drag force,
normalized bubble surface and Sherwood number for bubbles in a vertical pipe.

$$
\mathrm{Re}\sim O(100-1000)
$$

## 1. Introduction

Gravity-driven bubbly flow [1] is frequent in nature and industry.
Prominent applications can be found in thermal power plants and
industrial chemical processes. For example, bubble columns are used as
chemical or biochemical reactors [2] to synthesize chemical products
or to perform separation processes in the so-called unit operations
of chemical engineering. In many mass transfer devices, gas bubbles
are created by a large number of nozzles or by a perforated plate.
This subdivision of the gaseous phase into small bubbles aims to
increase the interface area and promote relative motion between the
phases. On the other hand, the design, optimization, and scale-up of
industrial-scale multiphase systems rely on the prediction of pressure
drop, heat and mass transfer rates, and chemical selectivity [3], which
are strongly related to the bubble fraction, bubble shape, and bubble
size distribution. Consequently, out of necessity or because of scientific
interest, developing predictive models for drag force and mass transfer
in bubbles becomes crucial.

Hydrodynamics and mass transfer in bubbly flows have been researched by experimental measurements [4–7], analytical solutions
of simplified mathematical models [8,9], and numerical and computational methods [7,10,11]. Analytical solutions are constrained to

exceptional cases with substantial simplification of physics. Moreover,
the multitude of spatial and temporal scales or limitations in optical
access pose technical barriers to experimental measurements. Indeed,
developing numerical and computational methods is well justified as
a complementary approach to unravel the complexities of transport
phenomena in bubbly flows.

Concerning numerical and computational methods, three approaches
are remarked [10–12]: Euler-Euler method (E-E) or two-fluid model [10,
13], Euler–Lagrange method (E-L) [10] and Direct Numerical Simulation (DNS) [10,11]. The E-E method considers the continuous and
dispersed phases fully interpenetrating, i.e., both phases occupy the
same space with different volume fractions and velocities. The E-E
method structure needs to know the closure relationships between
fluid phases. The E-L method formulates the continuous fluid phase
in the Eulerian framework, whereas the Lagrangian approach tracks
the position and velocity of bubbles or droplets (dispersed phase).
Moreover, the closure relations for the interaction forces between the
phases must be given. On the other hand, advances in supercomputers
have empowered DNS of two-phase flows, where fluid motion and
interface structures are solved from first physical principles, as a

