practical approach to design non-invasive and controlled numerical
experiments of bubbly flows [11,14–17].

Multiple methods have been reported for DNS of two-phase flows,
which can be categorized based on the underlying scheme used for
the advection of the gas-liquid interface, for instance: front-tracking
(FT) [10,11,14,18], level-set (LS) [19,20], Volume of Fluid (VoF) [21,
22], coupled VoF-LS [23–26], and conservative level-set (CLS) [27–29].
These methods solve two-phase flows in the framework of the so-called
one-fluid formulation [10,11], where the density and viscosity are
regularized across the interface, and surface tension is incorporated as
a singular source term in Navier–Stokes equations. VoF, LS, and CLS are
Eulerian methods in which the interface is captured using an implicit
function, e.g., colour function (VoF), signed-distance function (LS), and
regularized signed-distance function (CLS). On the other hand, FT is
a Lagrangian approach, where the interface is tracked explicitly by
connected marker points. Although these methods share a similar idea,
their numerical implementations on structured or unstructured meshes
are quite different [17,26,29].

Further research efforts have extended these methods to address
mass transfer in single bubbles. For instance, Davidson and Rudman
[30], Bothe et al. [31], Alke et al. [32], Onea et al. [33], Gupta et al.
[34] and Hayashi and Tomiyama [35] reported VoF simulations of
mass transfer of chemical species at deformable fluid interfaces. Bothe
et al. [36] and Francois and Carlson [37] reported VoF simulations
of conjugate reactive mass transfer using a variable transformation
technique to include concentration jumps at the interface into a single
continuous variable. Hayashi et al. [38] reported numerical simulations
of dissolving Taylor bubbles and experiments to validate the numerical method. A sub-grid-scale model for mass transfer in single rising
bubbles at high Schmidt numbers has been introduced by Bothe and
Fleckenstein [39] and Weiner and Bothe [40]. Claassen et al. [41]
further assessed a subgrid-scale mass transfer model for bubbles using
the front-tracking method. Marschall et al. [42] and Deising et al. [43]
have presented a Continuous Species Transfer (CST) model for mass
transfer in multiphase flow. Yang and Mao [44] and Wang et al. [45] introduced a level-set model for conjugate mass transfer in single bubbles
or droplets. Darmana et al. [46] reported a front-tracking model for the
computation of mass transfer coefficients in single-rising bubbles. An
arbitrary Lagrangian-Eulerian (ALE) Interface-Tracking algorithm for
reactive mass transfer in bubbles has been reported by Falcone et al.
[47].

Concerning experimental measurements of wall effects on the hydrodynamics or mass transfer in bubbles, Krishna et al. [48] reported
the velocity of the rise of air bubbles in the size range *𝑑*<sub>𝑏</sub>= 3−80 mm in
water using columns with inside diameters *𝐷𝛺* = 0*.*01 up to 0*.*63 m. Kurimoto et al. [49] researched terminal velocities and shapes of drops
rising through vertical pipes in clean and fully contaminated systems.
Mukundakrishnan et al. [50] reported a numerical study for the wall
effects on buoyancy bubbles at low Reynolds numbers (Re *<* 70). Abe
et al. [51] studied the dissolution of a carbon dioxide bubble in a
vertical tube. Aoki et al. [52] researched the mass transfer from single
carbon dioxide bubbles rising through contaminated water in a vertical
pipe of 12*.*5 mm, including various bubble shapes such as spheroidal,
wobbling, cap and Taylor bubbles. Hayashi et al. [38] researched the
effects of shape oscillation on mass transfer from a Taylor bubble in
a glycerol-water solution as well as validations of interface tracking
simulations. Hosoda et al. [5] reported mass transfer measurements
from single carbon dioxide bubbles in vertical pipes and developed a
mass transfer correlation for a wide range of bubble diameters.

$$
d_{b}=3-80
$$

$$
D_{\Omega}=0.01
$$

Few works on the DNS of mass transfer in bubble swarms can be
found in the literature. In this context, a multiscale approach for mass
transfer in bubbly flows that employs a boundary-layer approach close
to the bubble and a coarse grid for the rest of the flow was introduced
by Aboulhasanzadeh et al. [53,54],Aboulhasanzadeh and Tryggvason
[55], in the framework of the Front-Tracking method. Roghair et al.
[16] have reported a Front-Tracking method for mass transfer in dense

bubbly flows. Koynov et al. [3,56] and Radl et al. [57] researched the
impact of bubble swarm hydrodynamics on chemical reaction selectivity employing two-dimensional front-tracking simulations. Balcazar-
Arciniega et al. [17] introduced a novel multiple marker level-set
method for mass transfer in bubble swarms.

Concerning experimental measurements, Colombet et al. [6] researched the liquid-side mass transfer coefficient in a dense bubble
swarm for a wide range of gas volume fractions in an air–water system
in a square column. Colombet et al. [58] studied the collective effect
on bubble dynamics and mass transfer in a dense homogeneous bubble
swarm at a large gas volume fraction. Risso [7] reported a review
of numerical and experimental research on bubble dynamics and their
influence on interfacial transfers.

Although the previous works report remarkable findings on hydrodynamics and mass transfer in bubbles, multiple flow conditions and
geometrical configurations, e.g., wobbling bubbles [8] in vertical pipes,
still need to be explored. Additionally, it is worth noting that most of
the existing interface-capturing or front-tracking methods for hydrodynamics and mass transfer in bubble swarms are designed for structured
meshes, which limits their adaptability to complex geometries. To the
authors’ knowledge, drag force, bubble shape and mass transfer in
gravity-driven bubbly flow in a vertical pipe have yet to be researched
through the multi-marker unstructured conservative level-set (UCLS)
method [17]. Indeed, technical and scientific motivations arise because
several interface transport processes that involve bubbles are not accessible with experimental methods or have not been computed with
existing developed models. This research contributes to filling this gap
by performing a systematic numerical study of the wall effects on the
drag force, bubble shape and mass transfer in bubbles in a vertical pipe
through the multi-marker UCLS method. Consistently with this research
objective, novel correlations for the drag force coefficient, normalized
area and Sherwood number are proposed, taking into account the effect
of the confinement ratio.

Some advantages of the multi-marker UCLS method [17] are remarked. First, the mass conservation issue inherent to standard level-set
methods is circumvented [17], whereas the multiple-marker approach
avoids the numerical coalescence of bubbles [17,59]. Second, accurate
computation of surface tension forces on unstructured meshes without
reconstructing distance functions as in coupled volume-of-fluid/levelset methods or explicit interface representation as in front-tracking
methods. Moreover, most computational operations are local, which
permits an efficient implementation on parallel computational platforms [17]. On the other hand, unstructured meshes are easily adaptable to complex domains, enabling an optimized grid distribution in
regions where mesh refinement is necessary, e.g., close to the pipe wall.
Concerning the discretization, unstructured flux-limiter schemes proposed by Balcazar-Arciniega et al. [17] and Balcázar et al. [29] are used
to solve the convective term of transport equations. As a consequence,
the appropriate selection of these schemes avoids numerical oscillations
around discontinuities, whereas the so-called numerical diffusion is
minimized. This is relevant, especially in bubbly flows with highdensity ratios and high Reynolds numbers. In this framework, from the
numerical point of view this work contributes with the assessment of
unstructured flux-limiters convective schemes as applied in simulations
of mass transfer in bubbles at high Reynolds numbers. Finally, the
finite-volume methodology is attractive due to the satisfaction of the
integral forms of the conservation laws on the whole domain.

This paper is organized as follows: The mathematical model is
presented in Section2. Numerical methods are reviewed in Section3.
Numerical experiments are presented in Section4. Finally, concluding
remarks are discussed in Section5.

