## 4. Numerical experiments

Various experimental and numerical investigations [8,26,29,88,91]
have been focused on unconfined bubbles within finite-dimensional
containers. Indeed, wall effects have been a factor in these studies,
even though experiments or simulations conducted in large-diameter
containers have aimed to minimize these effects. On the other hand,
few works have researched the hydrodynamics and mass transfer in
bubbles in a vertical pipe, e.g., Hayashi et al. [38] and Clift et al. [8].
Consequently, many flow conditions still need to be explored.

In our previous work, Balcazar-Arciniega et al. [17] introduced,
verified, and validated a novel multi-marker UCLS method for mass
transfer in bubbly flows. Further developments and validations of
the UCLS method include gravity-driven rising bubbles [29,61,68,92],
binary droplet collision with bouncing outcome [59], drop collision
against a fluid interface [59], thermocapillary motion of droplets [67].
Building upon this robust foundation, the following numerical experiments research wall effects on interface area, drag force and Sherwood
number in bubbles in a vertical pipe.

## 4.1. Wall effects on single bubbles in a vertical pipe

