## 4.1.2. Effect of convective schemes: Unstructured flux-limiters

The convective scheme of transport equations is carefully designed
to avoid numerical oscillations around discontinuities and minimize numerical diffusion [64]. This research employs unstructured flux-limiter
convective schemes, Eq.(15), proposed by Balcazar-Arciniega et al.
[17] and Balcázar et al. [29], to discretize the momentum transport
equation (Eq.(1)), concentration equation (Eq.(20)) and interface
advection equation (Eq.(18)). This assessment is particularly important
for bubbly flows with a high-density ratio and high Reynolds number,
where accuracy and numerical stability can be strongly affected by the
convective scheme.

Flux-limiter functions outlined in Eq.(16)are assessed, i.e., UP-
WIND, MINMOD, SMART and SUPERBEE. CD limiter is excluded since
numerical instabilities were observed. A single rising bubble is simulated on the mesh *𝑀* (Table1), with Eo = 3*.*125, Mo = 10<sup>−11</sup>,
3,4
CR = 0*.*44, Sc = 1, Da = 0, *𝜂*<sub>𝜌</sub>= 100, *𝜂*<sub>𝜇</sub>= 100.Fig.9a depict
{Re(t<sup>∗</sup>)*,* A<sup>∗</sup>(t<sup>∗</sup>)*,* Sh(t<sup>∗</sup>)}, and their time-averaged values {Re*,* A<sup>∗</sup>*,* Sh}.

$$
M_{3,4}
$$

$$
E_{0}=3.125,\;M_{0}=10^{-11}
$$

$$
C_{R}=0.44,\ \mathrm{Sc}=1
=0,\eta_{\rho}=100,\eta_{\mu}=100
$$

The computed Reynolds number are Re = {1042, 1012, 1023, 1031}
for UPWIND, MINMOD, SMART, and SUPERBEE flux-limiter functions,
respectively. Concerning the Sherwood number,Fig.9-a shows that
the UPWIND scheme leads to a high Sh, which is promoted by the

numerical diffusion. MINMOD, SMART, and SUPERBEE flux-limiters
reduce the numerical diffusion, whereas the SUPERBEE scheme minimizes this effect.Fig.8a compares interface contours on the plane *𝑥* − *𝑦*
of *𝛺*.Figs.8b–c show concentration contours and vorticity contours
(*𝜔*<sub>𝑧</sub>= **𝐞**<sub>𝑧</sub>⋅ (∇×**𝐯**)) on the plane *𝑥* − *𝑦*. The numerical diffusion introduced
by the UPWIND scheme tends to smooth out the concentration contours
around the bubble. On the other hand, MINMOD, SMART and SUPER-
BEE schemes reduce these effects. Concerning the vorticity contours, a
similar observation applies.

$$
\left(\omega_{z}=\mathbf{e}_{z}\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf{v})\right)
$$

Accordingly, the SUPERBEE flux-limiter scheme is selected to minimize the numerical diffusion. Nevertheless, note that the SMART
limiter could be a second option. In contrast, the MINMOD limiter is
the third recommendation, taking the minimization of the numerical
diffusion effect as a selection criterion. These schemes preserve the
multiphase solver’s numerical stability in bubbly flows with a high
Reynolds number and high-density ratio.

## 4.1.3. Effect of density and viscosity ratios

The computational cost of simulations is strongly affected by the
ratio of physical properties. Indeed, as the density ratio increases, so
does the computational cost of solving Eq.(22). Consequently, the
choice of liquid-gas property ratios involves striking a compromise
between computational cost and their impact on the calculation of
{Re*,* Sh*,* A<sup>∗</sup>}. For this assessment, dimensionless numbers are set to
Eo = 3*.*125, Mo = 10<sup>−11</sup>, CR = 0*.*44, Sc = 1, Da = 0. The mesh *𝑀*
3,3
is used as described inTable1.

$$
