C_{\mathrm{D}}=\frac{4d_{b}(\rho_{c}-\rho_{d})g}{3\rho_{c}{\bf U}_{\mathrm{r}}^{2}}=\frac{4}{3\mathrm{Re}^{2}}\left(\frac{\mathrm{Eo}^{3}}{\mathrm{Mo}}\right)^{1/2},
$$

(33)

with *𝑔* = ‖**𝐠**‖. A drag correlation was proposed by Tomiyama et al. [93]
for clean bubbles on unconfined domains:
{ { ()}}

$$
g=\|\mathbf{g}\|.
$$

$$
\mathrm{C_{D}}=\max\left\{\min\left\{\frac{16}{\mathrm{Re}}\left(1+0.15\mathrm{Re}^{0.687}\right),\frac{48}{\mathrm{Re}}\right\},\frac{8}{3}\frac{\mathrm{Re}}{\mathrm{Re}+4}\right\}
$$

(34)

which takes into account the transition between the viscosity dominant
regime (spherical shape bubbles) and the surface tension and gravity
dominant regime (non-spherical bubbles) [93].

Dijkhuizen et al. [94] proposed a drag closure for pure liquids,
CD= CD(Re*,*Eo), valid for spherical and deformed bubbles rising in an
unbounded domain, which has the form:
√

$$
\mathrm{C_D}=\mathrm{C_D}(\mathrm{Re},\mathrm{Eo})
$$

(35)

$$
\begin{aligned}&\mathrm{C_{D}}=\sqrt{\mathrm{C_{D}(Re)}^{2}+\mathrm{C_{D}(Eo)}^{2}},\\&\mathrm{C_{D}(Re)}=\frac{16}{\mathrm{Re}}\left(1+\frac{2}{1+\frac{16}{\mathrm{Re}}+\frac{3.315}{\sqrt{\mathrm{Re}}}}\right),\mathrm{C_{D}(Eo)}=\frac{4\mathrm{Eo}}{9.5+\mathrm{Eo}}.\\ \end{aligned}
$$

(36)

Clift et al. [8] reported an empirical correlation for the wall effect on
CD:

$$
\mathrm{Re}>200,\;\mathrm{C_{D0}}
