The effect of Mo on the vorticity, concentration and bubble shape
is illustrated inFig.10, for 10<sup>−11</sup>≤ Mo ≤ 10<sup>−8</sup>, and Eo = {3*.*125*,*6}.
The generation of vorticity structures, promoted by bubble and wall
interactions and bubble shape oscillations, increases as Mo reduces,
or equivalently for higher Re. Furthermore, this bubble-induced turbulence promotes the mixing of chemical species in the continuous phase.
On the other hand,Fig.11shows the effect of the confinement ratio for
CR *<* 0*.*8, Eo = 4*.*5 and Mo = 10<sup>−11</sup>. In this case, the more CR is reduced,
the more generation of vorticity structures in the bubble’s wake, with
a consequent mixing of chemical species. Further illustrative videos
of these numerical experiments have been attached in the section on
additional material for a wide range of confinement ratios.

