Figs.12–14present the simulations performed in this research for
single bubbles in a vertical pipe, and depict the time evolution of
Reynolds number, normalized interfacial area and Sherwood number,
denoted respectively as {Re(t<sup>∗</sup>)*,* A (t<sup>∗</sup> <sup>∗</sup>)*,* Sh(t<sup>∗</sup>)}, for Eo = {3*.*125*,* 4*.*5*,*
6*.*0}, 10<sup>−11</sup>≤ Mo ≤ 10<sup>−8</sup>, 0*.*33 ≤ CR ≤ 0*.*8, Sc = 1 and Da = 0,
as computed by the UCLS method. In this framework, a decrement
of Morton number or increment of Eötvös number leads to a higher
Reynolds number, whereas the amplitude of oscillations in Re(t<sup>∗</sup>) tends
to be attenuated as the Morton number increases or confinement ratio
increases. The time evolution of the normalized bubble area (A )<sup>∗</sup>
presents oscillations. If the Eötvös number increases or Morton number decreases, the interfacial area increases. On the other hand, an
increment of the confinement ratio constrains the bubble deformation,
whereas the normalized interfacial area tends to unite, approaching a
spherical bubble. Note also that interfacial area oscillations tend to be
attenuated at higher Morton numbers and confinement ratios. Although
the hydrodynamics is not affected by the mass transfer, hydrodynamics

