(38)

The shape of an ellipsoidal bubble is characterized by the aspect
ratio (E), defined as the ratio of the maximum vertical dimension to
the maximum horizontal dimension. The surface ratio of an equivalent
sphere and the oblate ellipsoid (1∕A<sup>∗</sup>) can be expressed in terms of E:
√

$$
\frac{1}{\mathrm{A}^{*}}=\frac{4\mathrm{E}^{2/3}\sqrt{1-\mathrm{E}^{2}}}{2\sqrt{1-\mathrm{E}^{2}}+\mathrm{E}^{2}\ln\left(\frac{2\sqrt{1-\mathrm{E}^{2}}-\mathrm{E}^{2}+2}{\mathrm{E}^{2}}\right)}.
$$

In a pioneering work Wellek et al. [96] have introduced an empirical
relation for the prediction of E in drops with 6 ≤ Re ≤ 1354:

$$
6\leq Re\leq1354;
$$

$$
\mathrm{E}=\frac{1}{1+0.163\mathrm{Eo}^{0.757}}.
$$

Aoyama et al. [98] proposed an empirical correlation for E in a wide
range of fluid properties, 10<sup>−3</sup>≤ Re ≤ 130, −11 ≤ log(Mo) ≤ 0*.*63, and
0*.*042 ≤ Eo ≤ 29:
1

(39)

$$
10^{-3}\leq\mathrm{Re}\leq130,-11\leq\log(\mathrm{M}_{0})\leq0.63
$$

$$
0.042\leq\mathrm{Eo}\leq29;
$$

$$
\mathrm{E}=\frac{1}{\left(1+0.016\mathrm{E}\mathrm{o}^{1.12}\mathrm{Re}\right)^{0.388}}.
$$

(40)

Besagni and Deen [97] reported a correlation for the bubble shape
based on an extensive experimental data-set for −10*.*8 ≤ log(Mo) ≤ 2*.*3:

$$
\mathrm{for}-10.8\leq\log(\mathrm{M}_0)\leq2.3;
$$

$$
\mathrm{E}=\frac{1}{(1+0.45\left(\mathrm{E}\mathrm{o}\mathrm{Re}\right))^{0.08}}.
$$

(41)

Liu et al. [95] proposed a correlation for E under bubble swarm
condition:
{

$$
\mathrm{E}=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{\left(1+0.0255\left(\mathrm{Eo}\mathrm{Re}\right)\right)^{0.1572}}&,\mathrm{Eo}\mathrm{Re}<2300\\ 0.5263&,\mathrm{Eo}\mathrm{Re}\geq2300.\end{cases}
$$

(42)

Eqs.(39)–(42), lead to infer that E = E(Eo*,*Re) for ellipsoidal bubbles
on unconfined domains. Furthermore, A<sup>∗</sup>= f(E) = g(Eo*,*Re) according
to Eq.(38). Consequently, it is postulated that A<sup>∗</sup>= A<sup>∗</sup>(Eo*,*Re*,*CR)
= g(Eo*,*Re)h(CR) for bubbles in a vertical pipe:

$$
\mathrm{A^*=f(E)=g(Eo,Re)}
$$

$$
\mathrm{A^{*}\;=\;A^{*}(Eo,Re,C_{R})}
$$

$$
\mathrm{=g(Eo,Re)h(C_R)}
$$

$$
\mathrm{A}^{*}=(1+a_{1}\mathrm{E}\mathrm{o}^{a_{2}}\mathrm{Re}^{a_{3}})(1-\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{R}}^{a_{4}})^{a_{5}}.
$$

(43)

$$
a_{i}
$$

