Fig.21b shows the time-averaged normalized interfacial area (A<sup>∗</sup>)
versus the Morton number (Mo), including DNS data fromFig.20
against predictions from correlations proposed by Wellek et al. [96],
Aoyama et al. [98], Liu et al. [95], Besagni and Deen [97] and Eq.(38).
This assessment demonstrates an excellent agreement between DNS
data and correlations from the literature [95–98], whereas UCLS predictions are closer to correlations of Besagni and Deen [97] and Liu
et al. [95]. Consequently, these results validate the UCLS method for
the interfacial area of bubbles.

## 4.3. Mass transfer in bubbles in a vertical pipe

Concerning unconfined domains, multiple mass transfer correlations
have been proposed for spherical and ellipsoidal bubbles, as outlined
inTable2. Winnikow [100] reported a correlation ShPe<sup>−1∕2</sup>= f(Re)
for spherical bubbles based on the boundary layer theory, valid for
Re *>* 70. The gas dissolution process of a spherical rising gas bubble was
investigated experimentally and numerically by Takemura and Yabe
[102], leading to a correlation Sh = f(Re*,*Pe), valid for Pe *>* 1 and Re *<*
100. Lochiel and Calderbank [103] introduced a correlation ShPe<sup>−1∕2</sup>=
f(*𝜒*) for ellipsoidal bubbles at Re *≫* 1. Feng and Michaelides [99]

