Fig.20shows further UCLS simulations for Sh validation purposes,
with CR = 0*.*33. The time evolution of the Reynolds number, Sherwood
number and normalized interfacial area are remarked with black lines,
whereas their time-averaged values are remarked with red lines. At low
Eötvös number, Sh(t<sup>∗</sup>) tends to a well-defined steady state value, as
depicted inFig.20a. On the other hand, oscillations in the Sherwood
number are observed as the Reynolds number or the Eötvös number
increase, as depicted inFig.20b–c, which are induced by changes in
the bubble shape, bubble-wall interactions and path instabilities in the
wobbling regime [8].Fig.21a shows that {Sh*,* ShPe<sup>−1∕2</sup>} computed
by the UCLS method align consistently and closely with correlations
outlined inTables2and3, namely Feng and Michaelides [99], Winnikow [100], Takemura and Yabe [102], Lochiel and Calderbank
[103], Montes et al. [104], Abe et al. [51], and Cussler [101] (for
Re *>* 100). Consequently, these results validate the UCLS method for
mass transfer in bubbles, when CR effect is neglected.

