## 5. Conclusions

The UCLS method has undergone comprehensive validation for
parameters Re, CD, Sh, and A ,<sup>∗</sup>in the context of bubbles within a
vertical pipe. Upon this validation, an extensive series of 92 simulations
was executed, encompassing a parameter range of 3*.*125 ≤ Eo ≤ 6,
10<sup>−11</sup>≤ Mo ≤ 10<sup>−8</sup>, 0*.*33 ≤ C ≤ 0*.*8, with fixed Sc = 1 and Da = 0.
R
Concerning the normalized interfacial area of bubbles in a vertical pipe,
a novel correlation A<sup>∗</sup>= f(Eo*,*Re*,*CR) was proposed, as presented in
Eq.(43). Furthermore, novel correlations for mass transfer in bubbles
in a vertical pipe were proposed, namely: ShPe<sup>−1∕2</sup>= f(Eo*,*Re*,*CR),
ShPe<sup>−1∕2</sup>= f(Mo*,*Re*,*CR) and ShPe<sup>−1∕2</sup>= f(Gr*,*CR), as outlined in
Eq.(44). Finally, UCLS data was fitted to the drag force correlation
CD C = (1−CR<sup>𝑎</sup><sup>1</sup>)<sup>2</sup> <sup>𝑎</sup><sup>2</sup>, leading to {*𝑎,𝑎*} = {1*.*91*,*0*.*42}. These results
D0 −1 1 2
are in close agreement with the correlation proposed by Clift et al. [8]
(Eq.(36)).

